%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: phosphor.ps — /Phosphor, a video-terminal font on an 8x16 cell
%%Creator: pscat — issue #1426, the retro screen and printer faces
%%EndComments

% /Phosphor — the character set of an imagined 1980s video terminal:
% every printable ASCII character on an 8x16 cell (cap height 10,
% x-height 7, descenders 3, a gap column on the right), two-pixel
% vertical stems and one-pixel horizontals, a dotted zero. Each lit
% pixel is a square mark in the current colour, so
%
%   0.2 1 0.4 setrgbcolor          % green phosphor (1 0.7 0.1 is amber)
%   /Phosphor findfont 16 scalefont setfont
%   36 700 moveto (READY.) show
%
% puts one font pixel on one unit: at 16 points and 72 dpi the glyphs
% land on whole device pixels. /Phosphor-Scan is the same face with the
% dark gap between scan lines that a CRT shows; the gap needs at least
% two device pixels per font pixel (32 points at 72 dpi) to show.
%
% Dials (read from the font dict at BuildChar time; make a variant with
% the copy-and-definefont idiom, or pf-variant below, never by putting
% into a defined font, which Ghostscript makes read-only):
%   /DotShape  /square or /round           (default /square)
%   /DotSize   mark size, fraction of the pixel pitch (default 1)
%   /ScanGap   fraction of each pixel row left dark (square marks only;
%              default 0, /Phosphor-Scan 0.45)
%   /Interpolate  true adds a dot between neighbouring dots (round
%              marks only; default false)
%
%   /Phosphor-Wide /Phosphor << /DotSize 0.8 /DotShape /round >> pf-variant
%
% The bitmaps are readable: /Phosphor findfont /Bitmaps get /A get is
% the A's rows, top to bottom, '#' lit and '.' dark (cap top is row 0,
% the baseline sits under row 9), for a program that wants the raw
% pixel grid (an LED strip, a frame at true size).
%
% Provenance: an original design drawn for pscat in 2026 on this grid,
% not traced, converted or copied from any existing font or terminal
% character ROM. At this size several letters have only one legible
% form, so a likeness to other 8x16 faces there is forced by the grid.
% Licence: the same as the rest of pscat's lib/ (MIT OR Apache-2.0;
% THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md).
%
% Loading (#1492): run this file before the first findfont of its faces
% (a findfont before it silently gives the Helvetica substitute under
% that name), and run it once, in a program's setup, not per page or
% frame: preparing the glyphs takes some milliseconds. It registers its
% faces (each with /FontName, the name it is registered under) and
% leaves one name in the dictionary current when it runs, pf-variant;
% nothing else. A program with a pf-variant of its own runs the file
% inside a dictionary of its own:
%   10 dict begin (lib/fonts/phosphor.ps) run ... end
%
% /Bitmaps /space is one row of dark pixels as wide as the face's glyph
% box: the advance less the dark gap after a glyph (/Gap on the
% proportional faces, the last of the /Cell's 8 columns on the
% monospaced ones), so a program reading the rows needs no special case.
%
% Draws nothing on load; runs unchanged in Ghostscript.

40 dict begin   % the font dict: anonymous until definefont below (#1492)

  /FontType 3 def
  /FontName /Phosphor def      % the name it is registered under
  /FontMatrix [0.0625 0 0 0.0625 0 0] def   % 16 pixel rows to the em
  /FontBBox [0 -4 8 12] def
  /Cell 8 def                % advance, pixels (monospaced)
  /Proportional false def
  /Gap 0 def
  /Ascent 10 def             % row 0 of a bitmap is the cap top
  /DotShape /square def
  /DotSize 1 def
  /ScanGap 0 def
  /Interpolate false def

  /Bitmaps 100 dict def
  Bitmaps begin
    /.notdef [] def
    /space [(.......)] def
    /exclam [
